1. The Old Way (What you have now)
Trigger: GitLab sends a Webhook to your computer.

Execution: Your Python script (FastAPI) receives the webhook and runs the AI task.

Problem: It's hard to manage, not secure, and if your laptop goes to sleep, the agent is dead.

2. The Professional Way (What P'Benz wants)
You move the execution into the GitLab Runner, but you keep the trigger as a comment/assignment.

How it works:
The Trigger: A developer assigns an issue to the agent.

The Hook: GitLab Webhook sends a notification.

The Pipeline: Instead of your code doing the work, your server (or GitLab) triggers a Pipeline via the GitLab API Trigger.

The Runner: The Runner on your PC sees this pipeline and executes your agent's code inside a clean environment (a container).

Why this is better:
Stability: If the job fails, GitLab retries it. If the Runner is busy, it queues the job. Your script doesn't just crash.

Standardization: Everything stays inside GitLab. You don't have to worry about managing your own "always-on" server environment—GitLab handles the infrastructure.

Scalability: When you want to add more tasks (like checking code quality, security scanning, or generating docs), you just add new "Jobs" to that same pipeline.

Your Pivot
You do not need to change the logic of why the agent starts (it still starts when it sees a comment or assignment). You are changing how it starts.

Instead of your script "listening" and "executing," your script should "listen" and then "trigger a pipeline job."

Step A: Keep your FastAPI listener to catch the Webhook (Comment/Assign).

Step B: Inside the FastAPI route, write code that calls the GitLab API to trigger a pipeline (POST /projects/:id/pipeline).

Step C: Your .gitlab-ci.yml defines the job that the agent actually performs.
